    <h1>Faithfulness Judge</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">Measuring the measurer: can an LLM be trusted to tell you when an answer is making things up?</p>

    <p class="lead">
      Every LLM system I build is graded by another LLM at some point. That
      raises a question the grading quietly depends on and rarely answers: how
      good is the grader? This project measures that directly. It takes answers
      generated over public defense text, breaks them into individual claims,
      has a person label each one against its cited source, and then asks two
      model tiers to do the same job blind. The deliverable is not a working
      product. It is a number for how far the judge can be trusted, and an
      explicit statement of where it can&rsquo;t.
    </p>

    <h2>The result</h2>
    <table class="metrics">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Judge</th><th>Binary &kappa;</th><th>Raw agreement (95% CI)</th><th>Unsupported recall</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Opus</td><td>0.762</td><td>89.9% [84.8, 93.5]</td><td>97.9%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sonnet</td><td>0.716</td><td>88.4% [83.0, 92.2]</td><td>89.6%</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Both tiers land in what the statistics literature calls substantial
      agreement, which makes either usable as an automated faithfulness check.
      The tempting headline is that the premium tier wins. I didn&rsquo;t write
      that headline, because the confidence intervals overlap heavily at this
      sample size, so the gap in &kappa; isn&rsquo;t resolvable from this data.
      Reporting it as a win would be a soft number dressed up as a solid one.
    </p>
    <p>
      Those figures are measured against a gold set I audited in August, blind,
      under a selection rule committed before I looked at a single label. It
      held: <strong>28 of the 30 claims it pulled came back unchanged</strong>.
      That consistency is the finding. The audit also moved Opus from 0.751 to
      0.762, and <strong>that rise is not evidence the judge got better</strong>
      &mdash; restricted to the corrections that landed on claims neither judge
      had gotten wrong, it is 0.752, flat. The audit had reached one claim both
      judges failed, and removing it lifts agreement arithmetically rather than
      because anything about the judges changed. I pre-registered that check
      precisely so I couldn&rsquo;t talk myself into the more flattering reading
      afterward, and it is
      <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/faithfulness-judge/blob/main/decisions/002-solid-tier-call.md#phase-2-result-2026-08-02">published both ways</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The tempting second headline is recall on unsupported claims, where the
      split is <strong>97.9% versus 89.6%</strong>. It doesn&rsquo;t survive
      either, and the class it&rsquo;s measured over isn&rsquo;t the one the name
      suggests. Those percentages are 47 versus 43 catches out of 48, and that 48
      folds in hedged half-claims alongside outright fabrications. Restrict it to
      fabrication proper, the 36 claims the source flatly doesn&rsquo;t back, and
      the two tiers are <strong>identical</strong>: 35 of 36 each, missing the
      same claim, nothing between them. All four of the disagreeing pairs behind
      the gap are hedged claims, which the rubric itself calls the least crisp
      call a labeler makes. McNemar&rsquo;s exact test on those four gives
      <strong>p&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.125</strong>: the direction is consistent, with no
      reversals in 48 chances, but four pairs can&rsquo;t size an effect. An
      earlier version of this page reported that gap as the reason to pay for the
      premium tier, and called it recall on the fabrication class. The test and
      the restriction are the two corrections.
    </p>
    <p>
      So <strong>neither axis separates the tiers on this set</strong>. Paired
      across all 189 claims, the premium judge is right where the cheap one is
      wrong six times and wrong where it&rsquo;s right three times, which is
      <strong>p&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.51</strong>. The cheap tier is good enough and
      escalation isn&rsquo;t evidenced.
    </p>

    <h2>The result I almost published instead</h2>

    <div class="decision">
      <p class="tag">A truncation setting that looked like a finding</p>
      <p>The first scoring run showed Opus at 0.70 and Sonnet at
        <strong>0.43</strong>. A dramatic, clean tier gap, and a far more
        satisfying story than the one above: the expensive model is simply
        better at judging. It was one commit from shipping.</p>
      <p>It was false. I had set <code>max_tokens=10</code> to force a one-word
        verdict out of the judge. Opus complied. Sonnet tended to open with a
        short clause of reasoning first, so on <strong>39 of the 191 claims
        then scored</strong>,
        a full 20%, it was cut off before it ever reached the verdict word. My
        scoring counted every unparsed verdict as a disagreement, so a fifth of
        Sonnet&rsquo;s answers were scored as wrong when they were simply
        missing. On the 152 claims it actually finished, Sonnet was already at
        about 0.70, which is where it sits today.</p>
      <p>What caught it was reading the misjudgment log before publishing rather
        than after. Sonnet&rsquo;s failures didn&rsquo;t look like judgment
        errors, which are scattered and arguable. They looked
        <em>empty</em>. Errors have a shape, and that shape was wrong.</p>
      <p class="tradeoff"><strong>The lesson:</strong> when a model scores
        unexpectedly badly, inspect the raw outputs before believing the metric.
        A number can be perfectly reproducible and still be measuring your own
        harness. The generalization I actually changed my practice on: constrain
        a critical output <em>structurally</em>, not with a token budget.</p>
    </div>

    <p>
      The fix took two tries, and the failed one is worth recording. Prefilling
      the assistant&rsquo;s reply, seeding the response so the model continues
      from a fixed token, is the standard trick for forcing a format, and these
      models reject it. What works is a forced tool call: a
      <code>record_verdict</code> tool whose schema constrains the verdict to
      the label set, with the tool choice pinned so the model has to use it. The
      model can reason as long as it likes, and the verdict arrives in a
      structured block that cannot be truncated into ambiguity. That is the same
      pattern the classifier uses to guarantee valid labels, applied to a second
      problem.
    </p>
    <p>
      One scoring convention did the real work here, and it was tempting to
      change it. Counting unparsed verdicts as disagreements is harsh; dropping
      them would have been defensible and would have quietly discarded 20% of
      the set, hiding the truncation entirely and shipping the false gap. The
      strict rule is what made the bug visible. Full decision record in
      <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/faithfulness-judge/blob/main/decisions/001-both-tiers-substantial.md">ADR-001</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Why this is a different problem from the classifier</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href="defense-news-classifier.html">classifier</a> is graded
      against cheap, objective labels: a story is about procurement or it
      isn&rsquo;t, and two careful people will mostly agree. This project grades
      against expensive, subjective ones. Deciding whether a specific sentence
      is supported by a specific cited passage takes real reading, the
      boundaries are genuinely arguable, and a hedged half-claim is a judgment
      call rather than a lookup. It reuses the classifier&rsquo;s machinery, the
      judge harness, the Wilson intervals, the gold-set discipline, and points
      it at the opposite ground-truth regime.
    </p>
    <p>
      It also produced the third verdict in a row on the same question. The
      classifier retired BM25 grounding after measuring it, then declined tiered
      model routing after measuring that. Here the cheap tier is again close
      enough that escalation is hard to justify on overall agreement. Three
      independent measurements, all landing on <em>escalation barely pays</em>,
      is no longer a one-off result. It is the reason the default across this
      system is the cheaper model until an eval says otherwise.
    </p>

    <h2>What this number does not establish</h2>
    <p>
      The limits are on the repo&rsquo;s front page rather than buried at the
      bottom, because a floor is only useful if you state where it gives out.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>One labeler.</strong> The gold set is my labels alone, with no
        inter-annotator agreement measured. That makes the ground truth one
        person&rsquo;s consistent reading of a written rubric, not a validated
        consensus. A judge agreeing with this gold at 0.762 has not been shown
        to agree with humans in general at 0.762, and adding a second labeler is
        the single thing that would upgrade the claim. <strong>The August audit
        does not change this.</strong> It was the same person re-reading his own
        labels, blind to the verdicts, which measures whether the gold is
        self-consistent and cannot measure whether it is right. A consistency
        audit is never inter-annotator agreement.</li>
      <li><strong>The gold set has been audited twice, and moved both times.</strong>
        The first pass caught two claims labeled supported that were filler with
        no factual assertion, which the rubric excludes. The second, the blind
        one, found a third instance the first pass had missed &mdash; and
        reversed one of the first pass&rsquo;s own calls, where the claim does
        assert that the source omits a detail and so is a correct refusal rather
        than filler. The second audit is also the evidence that the first was
        incomplete. Single-labeler ground truth is worth auditing, not just
        declaring, and one audit is not obviously enough either.</li>
      <li><strong>189 scored claims</strong> out of 193, with four excluded as
        non-claims. The intervals are about nine points wide. Any difference
        smaller than that is noise, and that includes both the tier gap in
        &kappa; and the recall gap, whose denominator is only 48.</li>
      <li><strong>Floor tier by design.</strong> One pass, no judge prompt
        tuning, no ensembles, no retrieval. This measures the ruler as built,
        not the best ruler obtainable.</li>
      <li><strong>Short passages from one domain.</strong> Public defense
        reporting, skewed toward operations and procurement. Faithfulness
        judging over long documents or technical specifications is untested
        here.</li>
      <li><strong>A floor I&rsquo;ll say out loud:</strong> below about 0.6 I
        wouldn&rsquo;t trust an automated verdict unreviewed. Both judges clear
        that. Neither clears the bar for treating a verdict as final without a
        human in the loop on anything consequential.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Stack</h2>
    <p>
      Python, the Anthropic SDK (Sonnet and Opus as the two judges under test),
      forced tool-use for the verdict schema, Cohen&rsquo;s &kappa; and Wilson
      intervals computed directly, pytest on mocked calls. Source text is public
      DVIDS reporting; nothing proprietary or non-public is used anywhere in the
      project. The repo is public:
      <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/faithfulness-judge">github.com/sanlee-ys/faithfulness-judge</a>
      &mdash; the gold set, the labeling guide, the misjudgment log, and the ADR
      that documents the bug are all checkable.
    </p>

    <h2>What it demonstrates</h2>
    <p>
      Anyone can run an eval and report the number it prints. The transferable
      skill is knowing when the number is lying to you, and being willing to
      publish the boring true result after you&rsquo;ve seen the exciting false
      one.
    </p>
